A score of 85%, at five different sample sizes.

      n  passed  95% interval      width   can it see a 3-point gain?
     20      17  64.0% - 94.8%      30.8pt   no
     50      42  71.5% - 91.7%      20.2pt   no
    120     102  77.5% - 90.3%      12.8pt   no
    500     425  81.6% - 87.9%       6.3pt   no
   2000    1700  83.4% - 86.5%       3.1pt   yes

The interval shrinks with the square root of n, which is the single most
expensive fact in evaluation: to halve the width you need four times the cases.

At n=120, the two methods agree to within a point, as they should:

  Wilson      77.5% - 90.3%
  bootstrap   78.3% - 90.8%

Use Wilson for a simple pass rate. Use the bootstrap when the score is anything
more complicated than counting successes, because it does not care what shape the
statistic is.


Is 84% better than 81%? It depends entirely on n.

   n each  A               B               overlap?  proper test   verdict
       50  71%-92%         67%-89%         yes       p=0.603       nothing yet
      120  77%-90%         73%-87%         yes       p=0.497       nothing yet
      400  80%-87%         77%-85%         yes       p=0.264       nothing yet
     1500  82%-86%         79%-83%         yes       p=0.031       A is better
     3000  83%-85%         80%-82%         no        p=0.002       A is better
     6000  83%-85%         80%-82%         no        p=0.000       A is better

Two things to take from that table.

The first is how large n has to be. A three-point difference needs thousands of
cases before a proper test will call it, and almost nobody has thousands of cases.
That is not a reason to despair; it is a reason to stop arguing about three points.

The second is that 'do the intervals overlap' is much more conservative than the
test. It says 'nothing yet' well past the point where the test says otherwise — so
it is a safe instinct at a whiteboard and the wrong instrument for a decision.


And there is a much cheaper way: run both systems on the SAME cases.

  both correct          92
  both wrong            12
  only A correct        11   <- the signal
  only B correct         5   <- the signal

  A scores 86%, B scores 81% — a 5-point gap over 120 cases.
  But the comparison rests on only 16 cases, because the 104 where they
  agree carry no information about which is better.

  McNemar's exact test on those 16: p = 0.210 (not significant at 0.05)

Pairing removes the case-to-case variation that dominates an unpaired
comparison, and it is free: you were going to run both systems anyway.
